1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,925
This is the advanced database search lecture for KB8029, Bioinformatics, given by Petras Kundrotas.

2
00:00:07,925 --> 00:00:17,275
It covers three ways of searching a sequence database: word search with BLAST, profile search with PSI-BLAST, and profile hidden Markov models.

3
00:00:17,875 --> 00:00:25,400
This lecture draws on chapters four and five of the Pevsner book, with the topics rearranged a little so that they build on each other.

4
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:28,050
There are three parts.

5
00:00:28,050 --> 00:00:34,550
First, the word-search algorithm behind BLAST — the most widely used sequence search program there is.

6
00:00:34,550 --> 00:00:40,325
That is chapter four of the book, pages one hundred and twenty-eight to one hundred and forty-four.

7
00:00:40,325 --> 00:00:45,750
Second, sequence profiles, and why pseudocounts matter more than they look like they should.

8
00:00:45,750 --> 00:00:50,850
Chapter five, pages one hundred and seventy-one to one hundred and seventy-nine.

9
00:00:50,850 --> 00:00:55,900
Third, profile hidden Markov models: how to build one and how to use it.

10
00:00:55,900 --> 00:01:00,625
The book covers that only briefly, so most of it comes from this lecture.

11
00:01:01,225 --> 00:01:09,575
Last time we looked at dynamic programming — the algorithms that find the best possible global or local alignment between two sequences.

12
00:01:09,575 --> 00:01:13,325
They are exact, and they are guaranteed to find the optimum.

13
00:01:13,325 --> 00:01:15,575
The difficulty is scale.

14
00:01:15,575 --> 00:01:23,525
The databases hold somewhere between a billion and ten billion sequences, and a typical query is two to five hundred residues.

15
00:01:23,525 --> 00:01:30,125
Running an exact algorithm against all of that is a needle in a haystack, and in practice it cannot be done.

16
00:01:30,125 --> 00:01:32,300
There are three ways out.

17
00:01:32,300 --> 00:01:40,625
You can put the algorithm into hardware — graphics processors are built for exactly this kind of arithmetic — but that is expensive.

18
00:01:40,625 --> 00:01:48,050
You can spread the database over thousands of processors and combine the results afterwards, which is also expensive.

19
00:01:48,050 --> 00:01:50,625
Or you can use a heuristic.

20
00:01:50,625 --> 00:01:54,800
A heuristic is an algorithm that gives an approximate answer.

21
00:01:54,800 --> 00:01:58,175
Often you cannot prove the answer is the best one.

22
00:01:58,175 --> 00:02:05,600
What you get in exchange is speed, and the fact that it runs in software, so it costs nothing and everyone can use it.

23
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:09,600
The heuristics in this lecture rest on a few observations.

24
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:15,400
The database is updated rarely and searched constantly, so it pays to preprocess it.

25
00:02:15,400 --> 00:02:19,800
Substitutions are far more common than insertions and deletions.

26
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:28,275
And homologous sequences tend to contain stretches that match without any gaps at all — which makes those stretches a good place to start looking.

27
00:02:28,875 --> 00:02:33,325
Here is the whole algorithm in outline; the details come later.

28
00:02:33,325 --> 00:02:39,325
In the first stage, BLAST makes a list of short words and the words that score well against them.

29
00:02:39,325 --> 00:02:50,475
A word is a short fragment — three residues for proteins, usually longer for DNA and RNA — and the scores come from a substitution matrix such as BLOSUM62.

30
00:02:50,475 --> 00:02:55,275
In the second stage it searches the preprocessed database for those words.

31
00:02:55,275 --> 00:03:03,125
Any match is a hit, and a hit is extended outwards in both directions, with and without gaps, under rules we will come to.

32
00:03:03,125 --> 00:03:12,625
Because the program is only looking for the positions of particular words, it never attempts the vast majority of alignments, which is where the speed comes from.

33
00:03:12,625 --> 00:03:22,725
In the third stage it works out how surprising the alignment is, given the size of the database and the scoring matrix, and reports it with that statistic attached.

34
00:03:23,325 --> 00:03:30,600
Worth asking: why is BLAST still the default, when there are more sensitive programs around — jackhmmer, HHblits?

35
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:32,500
Partly history.

36
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:41,450
BLAST has been there since the early nineteen-nineties, and the major databases are built to be searched with it, so results are comparable across studies.

37
00:03:41,450 --> 00:03:43,400
Partly access.

38
00:03:43,400 --> 00:03:53,125
It runs in a browser at NCBI with nothing to install, it is documented exhaustively, and it is usually the first alignment tool anyone is taught.

39
00:03:53,125 --> 00:03:55,050
Partly coverage.

40
00:03:55,050 --> 00:04:02,600
It handles nucleotide against nucleotide, protein against protein, and the translated comparisons in between.

41
00:04:02,600 --> 00:04:06,100
And partly that for routine work it is good enough.

42
00:04:06,100 --> 00:04:13,000
The more sensitive programs earn their keep when you are hunting distant relatives or building protein families.

43
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:20,250
For comparing a sequence against a well-curated database, BLAST answers the question, and it answers it quickly.

44
00:04:20,850 --> 00:04:22,850
Start with the words.

45
00:04:22,850 --> 00:04:28,525
For proteins a word is three amino acids, which gives eight thousand possible words.

46
00:04:28,525 --> 00:04:40,725
The useful consequence: you can score every possible pair of words in advance and store the result — an eight thousand by eight thousand table, computed once from whichever substitution matrix you are using.

47
00:04:40,725 --> 00:04:43,475
Here, BLOSUM62.

48
00:04:44,075 --> 00:04:49,700
Each three-letter combination gets a number, so the table can be indexed by integers.

49
00:04:50,300 --> 00:04:54,600
Better still, the triplet itself can be the index — a hash key.

50
00:04:54,600 --> 00:04:57,500
That is what makes the lookup instant.

51
00:04:58,100 --> 00:05:03,225
And this is how a score in that table is worked out from BLOSUM62.

52
00:05:03,225 --> 00:05:09,375
Any substitution matrix would do: BLOSUM, the PAM series, or something of your own.

53
00:05:09,975 --> 00:05:14,875
The same calculation again, worked through from BLOSUM62.

54
00:05:14,875 --> 00:05:20,475
Any scoring matrix would serve here — BLOSUM, PAM, or one you build yourself.

55
00:05:21,075 --> 00:05:24,200
The first step is to take the query apart.

56
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:33,350
The sequence is cut into every overlapping word of three residues — V-T-A, T-A-L, A-L-W, L-W-G, and so on to the end.

57
00:05:33,350 --> 00:05:41,500
A query of three hundred residues gives about three hundred words, and those words, not the sequence itself, are what the search looks for.

58
00:05:42,100 --> 00:05:48,000
A word may occur more than once in a sequence, so the table lists every position it occurs at.

59
00:05:48,600 --> 00:05:50,825
Now the search itself.

60
00:05:50,825 --> 00:05:58,900
For each word in the query, BLAST looks up every position in every database sequence where a word scoring above the threshold occurs.

61
00:05:58,900 --> 00:06:01,650
The hash table makes that fast.

62
00:06:01,650 --> 00:06:07,325
Finding one is called a hit, and a hit triggers extension outwards in both directions.

63
00:06:07,325 --> 00:06:14,775
If the alignment that results scores above a threshold, it is called a high-scoring segment pair and goes into the output.

64
00:06:14,775 --> 00:06:21,125
The extension can use the Smith-Waterman algorithm from last week, but with one important change.

65
00:06:21,125 --> 00:06:26,750
Smith-Waterman only knows where the best alignment ends after the whole matrix has been filled.

66
00:06:26,750 --> 00:06:33,925
BLAST never fills the whole matrix — that is the entire point — so it needs some other way to decide when to stop.

67
00:06:34,525 --> 00:06:40,600
The rule is easiest to see on an example, and this one is borrowed from the book at the bottom of the slide.

68
00:06:40,600 --> 00:06:47,650
It uses two sentences instead of two sequences, which works because a sentence is also a string of letters.

69
00:06:48,250 --> 00:06:50,450
Spaces are ignored.

70
00:06:50,450 --> 00:07:00,575
The drop-off score records how far the running score has fallen below the best score seen so far; whenever the score reaches a new best, the drop-off goes back to zero.

71
00:07:00,575 --> 00:07:03,800
Here the maximum drop-off allowed is five.

72
00:07:03,800 --> 00:07:11,625
The first six letters are identical in both sentences, so the score climbs steadily and the drop-off stays at zero throughout.

73
00:07:12,225 --> 00:07:20,600
Now the question that decides how BLAST behaves: having found a word that matches, how far do you extend the alignment before giving up?

74
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:26,475
The sentence on screen stands in for a sequence, so you can read the score rather than compute it.

75
00:07:27,075 --> 00:07:35,300
Extension starts at the matching word and runs outwards, one position at a time, adding each position's score to a running total.

76
00:07:35,900 --> 00:07:39,775
While the residues keep matching, the running score climbs.

77
00:07:40,375 --> 00:07:43,650
A mismatch costs points, and the score falls.

78
00:07:43,650 --> 00:07:49,400
That on its own is not a reason to stop: a good alignment can survive a bad position.

79
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:57,100
So extension continues, and the score may recover — which is exactly why you cannot stop at the first mismatch.

80
00:07:57,700 --> 00:08:04,225
But if the mismatches keep coming, the score keeps falling, and the question becomes whether it will ever climb back.

81
00:08:04,825 --> 00:08:11,950
The rule is a drop-off: when the running score has fallen more than a set amount below the best score seen so far, stop.

82
00:08:11,950 --> 00:08:16,875
That threshold is a parameter, and it trades sensitivity against speed.

83
00:08:17,475 --> 00:08:21,375
And the alignment reported is not where extension stopped.

84
00:08:21,375 --> 00:08:28,575
It is trimmed back to the position where the score was highest — the best local alignment inside the region that was explored.

85
00:08:29,175 --> 00:08:33,100
Now the statistics, because a score on its own means nothing.

86
00:08:33,100 --> 00:08:37,825
Random quantities often follow a normal distribution — the Gaussian.

87
00:08:37,825 --> 00:08:40,750
The simplified form is on the slide.

88
00:08:40,750 --> 00:08:48,800
The normalised variable carries the mean, which for this distribution is also the median and the mode, and the standard deviation.

89
00:08:48,800 --> 00:08:56,200
The constant in front makes the area under the curve equal one, which it has to be if the value is going to be a probability.

90
00:08:56,800 --> 00:09:03,925
And the probability that the variable is below some value — minus one, say — is the integral up to that point.

91
00:09:04,525 --> 00:09:08,425
Here is the thing that makes sequence statistics interesting.

92
00:09:08,425 --> 00:09:16,050
If you compare a query against a set of random sequences of the same length, the scores do not follow a normal distribution.

93
00:09:16,050 --> 00:09:22,150
They follow an extreme-value distribution, which Karlin and Altschul proved in the original paper.

94
00:09:22,150 --> 00:09:24,225
The difference matters.

95
00:09:24,225 --> 00:09:29,800
The extreme-value distribution is asymmetric, with a tail that stretches to the right.

96
00:09:29,800 --> 00:09:38,900
The normal distribution is centred on zero; this one has its mean at about nought point five eight, and its median at about nought point three seven.

